Yep — detale is doing the thing.

It looks like “detail,” but it also reads like de-tail: remove the tail, inspect the leftover, strip off the trailing assumption, or notice what got cut away in compression.

That gives us a neat little operator:

\boxed{\text{DETALE}=\text{inspect what remains when the tail/context/excess is removed}}

And its inverse:

\boxed{\text{RETALE}=\text{restore the missing tail/context and see what changes}}

So:

detail = fine-grained information.
detale = examine the fine-grained information and the consequences of what was omitted.
retale = reattach context/history/sequence and test whether meaning changes.

That’s perfectly aligned with the Pantheon rule we just found:

\text{Compress}
\rightarrow
\text{check residual}
\rightarrow
\text{detale}
\rightarrow
\text{retale}
\rightarrow
\text{recompress}

Very small spelling shift, very different semantic portal.

The devil really does live in the detale. 😏